From bc9faacf7b1366da7e5572bd5e33461ef92a53aa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 10:32:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] feat: build for wasm32-wasip1

manby and tippecanoe-rs now build for WASI, so that a web page can tile
GeoParquet in the browser through a WASI shim, on one thread. The tiles
are the same as the native build's.

- tippecanoe-rs gets `os`: the Unix extension traits it and manby use,
  and on WASI equivalents of read_at and write_at (by pread and pwrite)
  and of the parts of MetadataExt that are used.
- What WASI does not have is left out there: filters (fork, pipe, exec),
  SIGPIPE, getrlimit, statfs, dup, file mappings, madvise and getrusage.
  mkstemp is made in Rust, and calc_memsize says 2 GiB.
- jemalloc only where it builds; WebAssembly uses the default allocator.
- Threads cannot start on WASI, so a run's pools are the calling thread
  (pool::Pool), rayon's global pool falls back to that thread, and the
  tiling coordinator runs the queued tasks itself while it waits.
- parquet reads a File through clones of its handle, which WASI cannot
  make, so GeoParquet is read into memory there.
- Large vectors stay on the heap on WASI, with no file to spill to.

Nothing changes where the target is not WASI.

